<description>
Collapse the scattered non-guide pixels orthogonally onto the cells immediately beside the straight guide bar.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One non-background color forms a one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical guide bar, possibly with gaps along the bar axis.
- The only other non-background color appears as isolated source pixels scattered on either side of the guide.

Rule
- Start from an otherwise empty background canvas and keep the guide bar fixed.
- If the guide is horizontal, each source pixel whose column contains a guide pixel moves to the row directly above or below that guide pixel, preserving its side of the guide.
- If the guide is vertical, each source pixel whose row contains a guide pixel moves to the column directly left or right of that guide pixel, preserving its side of the guide.
- Source pixels whose perpendicular projection would hit a gap in the guide are erased.

Key insight
- The guide bar defines the valid landing slots; the scattered pixels are not copied in place, but compressed along perpendicular sightlines to the nearest cells beside that guide.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "non-guide foreground pixels scattered on either side of the straight teal guide bar",
  "path": "orthogonal cardinal ray from each source pixel toward the guide row/column; valid only if the ray meets a guide pixel, stopping in the cell immediately before that guide pixel",
  "trajectory": "move only the source pixel to the stopping cell, merging duplicate landings",
  "transform": "keep the guide bar fixed; erase original source pixels and any source pixels whose ray meets a gap in the guide"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10-17 rows x 10-15 cols; output same size as input
  colors: background=0, guide=8, source=one other color (not 0 or 8)
  guide_bar:
    orientation: horizontal (1 row) or vertical (1 col)
    position: not on edge row/col; >=5 cells of space on each side [observed]
    coverage: 70-100% of the axis it spans; starts at index 0 of that axis [observed]
    gaps: 0-2 internal gaps, each 1-3 cells wide; guide segments are contiguous runs
  source_pixels:
    count: 11-15 isolated single pixels (no two adjacent)
    placement: scattered on both sides of guide, never on the guide line itself
    some project onto guide positions (valid), others onto gaps or outside span (erased)
    valid projections per side >= 2 [observed]
  output_rule:
    guide bar preserved unchanged
    each source pixel projects orthogonally onto the guide; if its target column/row has a guide pixel, it lands in the adjacent cell on the same side; otherwise erased
    multiple sources projecting to same output cell merge into one pixel

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid dims (10-17 x 10-15), orientation (H or V), 3 distinct colors (bg=0, guide=8, source)
    2. Place guide bar at position with >=5 cells on each side; choose coverage 70-100% starting from index 0, with 0-2 internal gaps of size 1-3
    3. Build output adjacency pattern; for each guide pixel, decide if side-A and/or side-B get a source pixel (total 6-12 output source pixels, at least 2 per side)
    4. For each output source pixel, scatter 1-2 input source pixels on the same side at random distances (>=2) along the same perpendicular line; this naturally produces valid projections and merges
    5. Add erased sources by placing pixels on gap columns/rows or outside the guide span (3-7 of these ensure non-trivial filtering)
    6. Verify all source pixels are isolated (no adjacency) and not on the guide line; re-place if needed
